Population: Adults with a discharge-home order at a participating hospital during the enrollment period, whether or not they return the survey
Eligibility: Age 18 years or older; Discharge home ordered at a participating hospital
Follow-up starts: Discharge-home order, after baseline measurement and before the navigation offer
Strategies: Offer standard transition navigation; Provide usual discharge support
Versions: Bedside transition plan and two scheduled navigator contacts within 30 days; No access to the transition-navigation offer
Outcome: Patient-reported transition score, as a share of the maximum score, from the survey sent on day 30 and closed on day 45; the score lies between 0 and 1
Horizon: 30 days after discharge
Intercurrent events: Use the transition score regardless of readmission; Analyze the offer regardless of completed contacts; The protocol scores death before day 30 as the worst transition score (composite strategy)
Interference unit: Individual patient
Rationale: The recorded baseline variables cover the measured common causes; Given the arm and the baseline variables, survey response carries no further information about the unobserved score (missingness at random); Standardized offer and version records support that each offer is one well-defined action, so a patient's recorded outcome is the outcome they would have had under the offer they got; Reserved navigator capacity and access controls support no interference
                            Causal question (ate)
